  "worked_solution": "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
